P(id='F5', sec='F', title='Large models in sports: the survey view', stage='evergreen', stamp='VERIFIED', date='2026-11-11',
  cues=['How fast is the field growing?', 'Who are large models built for?', 'What do they still get wrong?'],
  body=[{'t':'ul','items':['A 2026 survey reviewed **241 core papers** on large models in sports from January 2020 to July 2025; output grew from 1 paper in 2020 to 78 in 2024 [@W:survey26pdf] [@W:survey26acl].','It maps six stakeholder groups: athletes and trainers, coaches and educators, referees, researchers, fans and social media, and the sports industry [@W:survey26].','Fan-side applications include commentary generation (MatchVoice, AiCommentator, LiveCC), highlight generation and refereeing assistants (X-VARS) [@W:survey26acl].','Its warning: general models may understand the \u201cwhat\u201d of a sports video but struggle with the \u201cwhy\u201d, the nuance professionals recognise [@W:survey26].']}],
  stickies=[{'c':'blue','x':'Gap the survey names: why, not what. ALTER EGO\u2019s grades are explicitly about why.'}],
  summary='Large-model sports research is exploding, mostly on describing. Reasoning about decisions is the open frontier.', links=['F3','F7','M1'])
P(id='F6', sec='F', title='Large Event Models: football\u2019s GPT moment?', stage='budding', stamp='VERIFIED', date='2026-11-11',
  cues=['What is a Large Event Model?', 'How does it predict?', 'Could it simulate the road not taken?'],
  body=[{'t':'p','x':'A **Large Event Model** borrows the language-model idea: predict the next event in a match from the events so far, then keep sampling to simulate whole matches [@W:lemfine] [@W:lemml].'},
        {'t':'ul','items':['It predicts in stages: the next event\u2019s type, then its accuracy and whether it is a goal, then location, time to the next event and team [@W:lemlang].','A language-style version tokenises event data so one model learns the \u201clanguage\u201d of soccer, beating earlier versions on next-event-type accuracy using the public Wyscout data [@W:lemlang].','Fine-tuned versions estimate how a player would perform in a different team\u2019s context [@W:lemfine].']},
        {'t':'p','x':'{{For ALTER EGO: sample many continuations after \u201cshoot\u201d versus \u201cpass\u201d, and grade the pick on the simulated outcomes. Option-conditioned simulation, learned from data.}}'}],
  summary='Event models are the most promising engine for football counterfactuals. They must still pass the same calibration gate.', links=['G7','G3','L3'])
P(id='F7', sec='F', title='Live AI commentary, and its catch', stage='budding', stamp='VERIFIED', date='2026-11-11',
  cues=['How fast is live commentary now?', 'How was it trained?', 'What is the risk for us?'],
  body=[{'t':'ul','items':['**LiveCC** (CVPR 2025) trains a video LLM on streaming speech transcripts, interleaving words and frames by timestamp [@W:livecc].','Its 7B model commentates with under 0.5 seconds of latency per frame at 2 FPS [@W:livecc], and its authors report it beating 72B models on commentary quality in real-time mode [@W:liveccabs].','It was scored on a new benchmark, LiveSports-3K, using LLM-as-a-judge [@W:liveccabs].','The catch, in its own demo: it reports a player\u2019s name from world knowledge picked up watching YouTube [@W:livecc].']}],
  stickies=[{'c':'pink','x':'Names from memory are exactly what our claim verifier must block. Every name needs a receipt.'}],
  summary='Real-time language is solved enough. Real-time truth is not. Pair fast models with slow, strict verification.', links=['G8','F3','A3'])
